From paul.schwarz at oregonstate.edu  Mon Jul 15 05:28:11 2002
From: paul.schwarz at oregonstate.edu (Schwarz, Paul)
Date: Sun, 14 Jul 2002 22:28:11 -0700
Subject: Vectorizing the calculation of pairwise differences
Message-ID: <6B677297622ED311A8E100902785F931059F650D@ribes>

S-News readers,

For an n x n matrix, how could I vectorize the calculation of all the pairwise differences among the matrix elements such that the resulting symmetric matrix would then be n^2 x n^2?  For example, if the matrix is 3 x 3, the resulting matrix would be 9 x 9 and contain the elements xi - xj for all pairs of i,j.

Thanks for your help.

-Paul

From Bill.Venables at CMIS.CSIRO.AU  Mon Jul 15 05:49:36 2002
From: Bill.Venables at CMIS.CSIRO.AU
Date: Mon, 15 Jul 2002 15:49:36 +1000
Subject: Re: Vectorizing the calculation of pairwise differences
Message-ID: <E09E527B56BE2D438A3D6A246DDD27A9165202@Roper-CV.qld.cmis.csiro.au>

Let 
X be the matrix.  Then the matrix of all pairwise differences of elements 
is

 

A 
<- outer(as.vector(X), as.vector(X), "-")

 

This is an n^2 x n^2 matrix but it is not symmetric - in fact 
it's skew-symmetric: A = -t(A).  

In 
particular the diagonal is zero.

 

Bill Venables.

