> example of Bate and Watts (1988) Nonlinear Regression Analysis 
> and Its Applications (the data are listed at the end of the message for 
> completeness). My data, however, contain four levels under the 'state' 
> variable, unlike Bate and Watts' two levels, so that I can fit two 
> additional regression models, i.e., fm3 and fm4 (fm1 and fm2 are 
> those given as the example in the book) as below:
> 
> fm1 <- nls(rate ~ Vm * conc/(K + conc), data = Puromycin,
>            subset = state == "treated",
>            start = c(Vm = 200, K = 0.05), trace = TRUE)
> fm2 <- nls(rate ~ Vm * conc/(K + conc), data = Puromycin,
>            subset = state == "untreated",
>            start = c(Vm = 160, K = 0.05), trace = TRUE)
> fm3 <- nls(rate ~ Vm * conc/(K + conc), data = Puromycin,
>       subset = state == "treat2",
>             start = c(Vm = 200, K = 0.05), trace = TRUE)
> fm4 <- nls(rate ~ Vm * conc/(K + conc), data = Puromycin,
>       subset = state == "treat3",
>             start = c(Vm = 200, K = 0.05), trace = TRUE)
        [WNV]  There is a curiously little-known method for fitting them all
in one:

        fm <- nls(rate ~ Vm[state] * conc/(K[state] + conc), data =
Puromycin,
                start = list(Vm = c(200, 160, 200, 200), K = rep(0.05, 4),
trace = TRUE)

        This is not quite equivalent to the four separate fits as it pools
the four separate estimates of residual variance rather than provide the
four separate estimators.  It does, however, simplify the next step
considerably.

> I need to statistically compare the estimates for the parameters Vm 
> and K between the four models. I can do this for two models, but with 
> four it seems somewhat more tricky. Can anyone help?
        [WNV]  Fit the sub-models with either Vm or K, or both, held
constant over state and do a large sample F-test by comparing changes in
residual variation.  There is an anova method for nls objects that will do
this for you, now, though back in the old days (i.e. the mid 90s) you had to
do it by (gulp) hand.

        [By the way, Doug's surname is "Bates", not "Bate".  Not sure how he
feels about this but I detest "Venable"...]

        Bill Venables.

