From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri Jul 26 11:35:47 2002
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk
Date: Fri, 26 Jul 2002 12:35:47 +0100
Subject: Re: mixed hierarchical models for binomial data
In-Reply-To: <D43231BBCF5D704DAB53E67175800F561A0B96@zcefe1.incefe.cnrsmntp.fr>
References: <D43231BBCF5D704DAB53E67175800F561A0B96@zcefe1.incefe.cnrsmntp.fr>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0207261226000.22425-100000@gannet.stats>

On Fri, 26 Jul 2002, Sandrine DEBAIN wrote:

> Hello,
>
> I would like to know the procedure to perform a mixed hiarchical model
> (such as lme) when the response vraiable is binomial?
> The model would be: (failed/success)~treatment+treatment nested with
> block, with block declared as a random effect.
> It seem than the function lme does not allow to declare a family for the
> response variable, and is restricted to the analysis of gaussian
> response variable.

It's possible, especially as that is not I think hierarchical (you only
have one level of random effect).  You need a generalized linear mixed
model (glmm).  Fitting such a model is a lot harder (conceptually and
computationally) than a linear mixed model.  One approach, PQL, is
implemented in function glmmPQL in the latest version of the MASS library
(S+6 only, but there is a version in the final updater for S+2000,
MASS version 6.99).  Another implementation of PQL is Gordon Smyth's
reglm (e.g. www.statsci.org/s/index.html) for S+2000.

