From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Sun Jul 28 17:34:48 2002
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk
Date: Sun, 28 Jul 2002 18:34:48 +0100
Subject: Re: contrasts and ANOVA
In-Reply-To: <200207281642.LAA31821@uhddx01.dt.uh.edu>
References: <200207281642.LAA31821@uhddx01.dt.uh.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0207281810350.385-100000@gannet.stats>

Erin,

To get linear and quadratic lines you need to use contr.poly() and/or an
ordered factor.  It's not so easy to see in your example (because of the
pattern of group means), so let's use random data:

set.seed(123)
xa <- rnorm(12)
wheat.df <- data.frame(y=xa, treat=as.factor(xb))
wheat.aov <- aov(y~treat,data=wheat.df)
summary(wheat.aov,data=wheat.df,split=list(treat=list(L=1,Q=2)))
           Df Sum of Sq  Mean Sq  F Value     Pr(F)
     treat  2  4.070704 2.035352 2.793324 0.1138391
  treat: L  1  0.183002 0.183002 0.251153 0.6282925
  treat: Q  1  3.887702 3.887702 5.335495 0.0462464
 Residuals  9  6.557840 0.728649

contrasts(wheat$treat) <- contr.poly(3)
wheat.aov <- aov(y~treat,data=wheat.df)
summary(wheat.aov,data=wheat.df,split=list(treat=list(L=1,Q=2)))
           Df Sum of Sq  Mean Sq  F Value     Pr(F)
     treat  2  4.070704 2.035352 2.793324 0.1138391
  treat: L  1  2.231053 2.231053 3.061904 0.1140743
  treat: Q  1  1.839652 1.839652 2.524744 0.1465359
 Residuals  9  6.557840 0.728649
summary.lm(wheat.aov)$coef
                  Value Std. Error   t value  Pr(>|t|)
(Intercept) -0.04472718  0.2464158 -0.181511 0.8599886
    treat.L -0.74683542  0.4268047 -1.749830 0.1140743
    treat.Q -0.67816879  0.4268047 -1.588944 0.1465359

The second version is the correct split into linear and quadratic. The
same idea works for any other contrasts: the `treat=list(L=1,Q=2)' says
you want the SSq for the first contrast labelled `L' and the second
labelled `Q'.  (NB: these are type 1 SSqs and so in general
order-dependent, but not so for orthogonal polynomials with balanced
designs.  Some care is needed to produce valid tables in general.)

Brian

