From Bill.Venables at CMIS.CSIRO.AU  Wed Jul 31 01:32:50 2002
From: Bill.Venables at CMIS.CSIRO.AU
Date: Wed, 31 Jul 2002 11:32:50 +1000
Subject: Re: How to handle BIG matrices
Message-ID: <E09E527B56BE2D438A3D6A246DDD27A91652E1@Roper-CV.qld.cmis.csiro.au>

Your assumption that to perform principal components analysis you need the
covariance matrix is not correct.  You only need that if you want to do it
using the usual tools, but the computation itself is very simple and can be
done from the data matrix directly.  In your case that definitely pays off.

Here is a "poor person's PCA".  It gets you the correct results but not the
frills. 

ppPCA <- function(X, correlations = FALSE) {
        Xsvd <- svd(scale(X, scale = correlations))
        list(variances = Xsvd$d^2, loadings = Xsvd$v, scores = Xsvd$u)
}

With a bit of tweaking you can make the object conform to the output of one
of the standard pca functions and hook into the summary, print, plot, &c
methods.  Exercise for reader.

