From elmore at nssl.noaa.gov  Wed Jul 31 16:09:25 2002
From: elmore at nssl.noaa.gov (Kim Elmore)
Date: Wed, 31 Jul 2002 11:09:25 -0500
Subject: Summary: How to handle BIG matrices
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.2.20020731103416.01ba6f08@172.16.3.4>

Dear S-Masters:

As is typically the case, the list has provided a wealth of information and 
insight. Thanks to all for your excellent replies.  The simple answer to my 
question is: S-Plus has no way aroundthis, but linear algebra does. I 
obviously slept through part of my linear algebra classes, as the optimal 
answer (for my purposes) was given by Richard Jarrett, who gently reminded 
me that, for X an n x m matrix, the first n eigenvalues (not so the 
eigenvectors) are identical for X'X and XX'. Clearly, for my case, m >> n, 
so the m x m matrix resulting from the X'X operation would not be full rank 
(at most, it will be rank n).  Incidentally, I'm using the PCA to help 
determine spatial degrees of freedom for a geostatistical field, so the 
eigenvalues are all I need.

Bill Venables also pointed out that I can perform the PCA on the data 
matrix itself using SVD, and even authored a neat function to do just 
that.  Brian Ripley then pointed out that the MASS prcomp() does almost 
exactly this, though the columns need to first be centered.  For a 400 x 
400 matrix on my machine, which has 2 GB of memory, the SVD executes very 
quickly (about 5 s).  As an aside, I've found that svd() yields generally 
better results than does eigen().

Thanks also to Bob Pruzek, Tony Plate, Patrick Burns, Alan Zaslavsky, 
Michael Meyer, Ryan Keane, and Robert Garrett.

My original question:

> I'm running S-Plus 6.1 under Windows 2000 on a dual-Athlon mcahine with 2
> GB or RAM. I have need to perform a PCA on a very large matrix (403 rows
> by 13272 columns), which means I need to somewhere deal with a 13272 x
> 13272 covariance matrix.  Is it possible to do this within S-Plus?  In
> double precision (64 bit numbers instead of 32 bit numbers) such an object
> exceeds the 2 GB limit of addressable memory within Windows 2000.  I've
> tried performing the matrix manipulations explicitly, but still get the
> dreaded "Unable to obtain requested dynamic memory" error message.  Within
> options() I've set $object.size to Inf, and tried increasing $memory above
> 2147483647, both to no avail.  Is there a way around this in S-Plus?

The responses I found most useful are given below:

from Richard Jarrett:

> Easy to do.
> When you do a PCA, the eigenvalues of X'X are the same as those of
> XX'(except for the 13272-403=12869 eigenvealues that are zero in the former
> case).  Hence you can do PCA on the 403x403 matrix, and get the eigenvalues
> and eigenvectors for XX'.  There will be 403 of them.and easily convert the
> eigenvectors u of XX' to the eigenvectors X'u for X'X s
> Since  (XX')u = a.u implies that X'(XX')u=(X'X)(X'u)=a(X'u), it follows that
> the 403 eigenvectors of X'X corresponding ot the nonzero eigenvalues are
> simply X'u, where u is the eigenvector of XX'.

from Bill Venables:

> Your assumption that to perform principal components analysis you need the
> covariance matrix is not correct.  You only need that if you want to do it
> using the usual tools, but the computation itself is very simple and can be
> done from the data matrix directly.  In your case that definitely pays off.
>
> Here is a "poor person's PCA".  It gets you the correct results but not the
> frills.
>
> ppPCA <- function(X, correlations = FALSE) {
>         Xsvd <- svd(scale(X, scale = correlations))
>         list(variances = Xsvd$d^2, loadings = Xsvd$v, scores = Xsvd$u)
> }
>
> With a bit of tweaking you can make the object conform to the output of one
> of the standard pca functions and hook into the summary, print, plot, &c
> methods.  Exercise for reader.
>
>
> Warning: code composed at the keyboard and not tested!

>From Brian Ripley (who quoted Bill Venables):

> Function prcomp() does almost exactly this: you need to remove the column
> means, BTW. However, that still needs a lot of memory, and would not run
> on my 512Mb without excessive paging.  I think you will find the svd is
> pretty slow.

Again, thank you all very much.

